  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and contact information for different offices and resources related to the United States House of Representatives and the United States Senate. The House of Representatives websites include the main House.gov website and the restricted HouseNet portal, which provides access to various House offices, leadership offices, committees, and support offices. The HouseNet portal includes access to the Clerk of the House, the House Legislative Resource Center, the House Legislative Counsel, the House Technology Help Desk, and other offices.\n\nThe Clerk of the House assists with archiving records, introducing publications, and providing assistance with legislative procedures. The House Legislative Resource Center provides access to current and historical House documents, including bills, reports, and other publications. The House Technology Help Desk offers technical assistance with computer systems and databases.\n\nThe Senate website includes the main Senate.gov website and the restricted Senate Webster portal, which provides access to various Senate offices, committees, and support offices. The Senate Webster portal includes access to the Secretary of the Senate, the Senate Legislative Resource Center, the Senate Legislative Counsel, the Senate Technology Help Desk, and other offices.\n\nThe Senate Secretary's office assists with ordering publications and provides information on current and historical Senate documents. The Senate Legislative Resource Center offers access to current and historical Senate documents, including bills, reports, and other publications. The Senate Technology Help Desk offers technical assistance with computer systems and databases.\n\nBoth the House and Senate websites offer access to Congress.gov, which provides timely, accurate, and complete legislative information, including bills, votes, and committee information. Congress.gov is a public website that replaced the THOMAS system in 2016.\n\nThe report also lists various external resources, including the Congressional Research Service, the Government Publishing Office, the Law Library of Congress, the National Archives and Records Administration, and the Office of Management and Budget. These resources offer a range of services, including legislative research, document reproduction, and access to newly enacted laws and executive orders.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and contact information for different offices and resources related to the United States House of Representatives and the United States Senate. The House of Representatives websites include the main House.gov website and the restricted HouseNet portal, which provides access to various House offices, leadership offices, committees, and support offices. The HouseNet portal includes access to the Clerk of the House, the House Legislative Resource Center, the House Legislative Counsel, the House Technology Help Desk, and other offices.\n\nThe Clerk of the House assists with archiving records, introducing publications, and providing assistance with legislative procedures. The House Legislative Resource Center provides access to current and historical House documents, including bills, reports, and other publications. The House Technology Help Desk offers technical assistance with computer systems and databases.\n\nThe Senate website includes the main Senate.gov website and the restricted Senate Webster portal, which provides access to various Senate offices, committees, and support offices. The Senate Webster portal includes access to the Secretary of the Senate, the Senate Legislative Resource Center, the Senate Legislative Counsel, the Senate Technology Help Desk, and other offices.\n\nThe Senate Secretary's office assists with ordering publications and provides information on current and historical Senate documents. The Senate Legislative Resource Center offers access to current and historical Senate documents, including bills, reports, and other publications. The Senate Technology Help Desk offers technical assistance with computer systems and databases.\n\nBoth the House and Senate websites offer access to Congress.gov, which provides timely, accurate, and complete legislative information, including bills, votes, and committee information. Congress.gov is a public website that replaced the THOMAS system in 2016.\n\nThe report also lists various external resources, including the Congressional Research Service, the Government Publishing Office, the Law Library of Congress, the National Archives and Records Administration, and the Office of Management and Budget. These resources offer a range of services, including legislative research, document reproduction, and access to newly enacted laws and executive orders.",
  "score": 19.89528752336834,
  "answers": [
    "This report provides a brief list of key House, Senate, legislative support agencies, and executive branch offices, as well as links to online resources of use to new congressional staff who work with legislative procedures and conduct legislative research. Some of the websites listed are available only to congressional offices; other sites are restricted by chamber and are only available to those staff working in either House or Senate offices. This report is intended for congressional use only and will be updated annually."
  ],
